course: dictionary
lesson: 11528
title: Allotment: land or a share
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An allotment has two meanings. In the UK, Ireland and Australia, an allotment is a small piece of land that people rent to grow vegetables, fruit and flowers. «Mum rents an allotment at the edge of town, where she grows carrots and beans.»
explain: The clues are the words around it: rents, grow, carrots and beans. They tell you the land is for growing plants, not for building a house. Always test your meaning by putting it into the sentence.
explain: The second meaning is more formal: an allotment can be a share or amount of something given out. «Each team got an allotment of five minutes to speak.» The clue is given out. Each team gets its own part, like a share of time.
fact: An allotment can mean a small piece of rented land for growing vegetables, fruit and flowers.
fact: In formal language, an allotment means a share or amount of something given out.
fact: In the UK, Ireland and Australia, many families keep an allotment to grow their own food.
fact: When you meet allotment, check the clues: is someone growing things, or is something being shared out?
exercise: «Dad grows potatoes and onions on his allotment.» What is an allotment in this sentence? | *A small piece of land for growing food | A type of tractor | A shop that sells seeds | A large dog kennel
exercise: «The teacher gave each group an allotment of ten minutes.» What does allotment mean here? | *A share of time given out | A garden shed | A long walk outside | A quiet corner of the classroom
exercise: «Ben's allotment has rows of carrots and strawberries.» What is grown on Ben's allotment? | *Carrots and strawberries | Cars and buses | Wooden toys and bikes | Bricks and a fence
test: «Priya rents an allotment to grow tomatoes.» What does allotment mean? | A sort of cake | A school bus | A pet shop | *A small piece of land for growing plants
test: «Each child was given an allotment of two stickers.» What does allotment mean here? | A brand of trainers | *A share of something given out | A holiday trip | A cupboard for books
exam: A maths quiz says, «Each player gets an allotment of six cards.» What does allotment mean? | A quiet room for thinking | A card game with rules | A sort of table | *A share given to each player
